{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you.</b> Some disagree with our holy forefathers who said that 'for a sign and for a remembrance' is in the manner of 'for they are a graceful wreath upon your head, and necklaces upon your throat' (Proverbs 1:9); likewise 'and you shall bind them for a sign upon your hand' (Deuteronomy 6:8), like 'bind them always upon your heart' (Proverbs 6:21); likewise 'and you shall write them upon the doorposts of your house' (Deuteronomy 6:9), like 'write them upon the tablet of your heart' (Proverbs 3:3). And what is it that shall be 'for a sign and for a remembrance'? That it be fluent in your mouth, 'for by a strong hand YHWH brought you out of Egypt.' And this is not a correct approach, for at the beginning of the book it is written 'the proverbs of Solomon,' and behold all that he mentioned is in the manner of a proverb. But it is not written in the Torah that it is in the manner of a proverb — far be it — rather it is according to its plain meaning. Therefore we do not remove it from its simple sense, for when it is according to its plain meaning it does not contradict reasoned judgment, unlike 'and you shall circumcise the foreskin of your heart' (Deuteronomy 10:16), which we are required to adjust according to reason:" "And Rabbi Moses the Kohen said that in most verses 'hand' is the left hand, as in 'My hand also has founded the earth, and My right hand has spanned the heavens' (Isaiah 45:13 [English 45:12]), 'she put her hand to the tent-peg' (Judges 5:26). And the words of the Prophets [lit. 'words of tradition'] are strong and do not require reinforcement."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:10.519735451Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}